From 618d592e45d2d3d3526d7a185579c3a4c2d63331 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 01:38:12 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-snd: recover from a failed control request and give the AudioUnit start room under load

Seen live on merge-1.9 (lean runs A_N19_DX20 / A_N19_VK20): "prepare took more than 2s;
failing it so the guest recovers", then 100+ guest lines "SID 0: invalid I/O queue state"
and lower fps; the 1.8.2 + 0021 runs had none.

Cause (offline): the 2 s control deadline of 0021/0022 fired on a PREPARE under load, and
the failure left the stream's queued I/O held by the device. The guest driver refuses
PREPARE while any of its I/O messages is in flight and the device only completes held
messages at PREPARE (or once playing), so nothing could ever release them: the storm.
It is not slow code: with the real default output device, an idle Mac does
configure/start/stop/release in 2-30 ms (real_device_cycle_timing, ignored test) and
output_route() in 0.2 ms; upstream's multichannel route code does not change that. The
runs were under memory/CPU pressure with the game's audio already underrunning, where a
HAL start can take seconds.

Upstream state (libkrun 1.19.6 + steamac 0017): no control deadline exists upstream; this
is our own 0022 mechanism.

Changes (CoreAudio backend):
* CTL_DEADLINE 2 s -> 8 s: a rescue for a call that never returns, not a latency target;
  still below the launcher's virtio_snd.msg_timeout_ms of 10 s.
* PREPARE/START/RELEASE that fail (including the deadline) complete the stream's queued
  I/O with IO_ERR (Inner::fail_stream_io), so the guest's next PREPARE is accepted.
* A unit request slower than 250 ms logs where the time went (unit calls, waiting for the
  pump's lock, latency refresh), so the next live run shows whether coreaudiod or the pump
  was slow.

Tests (cargo test --features gpu,snd --lib -- virtio::snd, 22 pass): a failed PREPARE
releases the queued I/O (fails with the recovery disabled); the deadline leaves room for a
loaded HAL; the existing stuck-pump tests still pass. real_device_cycle_timing is an
ignored manual timing of the real device.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

